. Creating an Object with Key-Value Pairs
js
Copy
Edit
const person = {
  name: "Alice",
  age: 30,
  isStudent: false
};
Keys: name, age, isStudent

Values: "Alice", 30, false

🔹 2. Accessing Values
Dot Notation:
js
Copy
Edit
console.log(person.name);    // "Alice"
console.log(person.age);     // 30
Bracket Notation:
js
Copy
Edit
console.log(person["name"]); // "Alice"
✅ Use bracket notation when:

The key is dynamic (from a variable)

The key has spaces or special characters

🔹 3. Updating Values
js
Copy
Edit
person.age = 31;               // Dot
person["isStudent"] = true;    // Bracket
🔹 4. Adding New Key-Value Pairs
js
Copy
Edit
person.city = "New York";         // Dot
person["country"] = "USA";        // Bracket
🔹 5. Deleting a Property
js
Copy
Edit
delete person.age;          // Dot
delete person["isStudent"]; // Bracket